WEBVTT

00:00.500 --> 00:02.070
We're on part b.

00:02.070 --> 00:05.960
The region R is rotated about
the x-axis to form a solid.

00:05.960 --> 00:09.120
Find the volume, V, of
the solid in terms of k.

00:09.120 --> 00:11.060
So this is our
region R, and it's

00:11.060 --> 00:13.680
going to be rotated
around the x-axis.

00:13.680 --> 00:17.120
It's going to be rotated around
the x-axis to form a solid.

00:17.120 --> 00:19.860
So that solid is going to
look something like this.

00:19.860 --> 00:21.110
It's going to look like this.

00:21.110 --> 00:22.818
This is going to be
this end of the solid

00:22.818 --> 00:25.350
when it's rotated
around, and it's

00:25.350 --> 00:27.760
going to look
something like this.

00:27.760 --> 00:32.540
Let me do my best attempt
to draw our solid.

00:32.540 --> 00:35.340
And then that side is going
to look something like that.

00:35.340 --> 00:37.830
It'll look a little
bit like a loudspeaker.

00:37.830 --> 00:39.996
And so let me shade
it in a little bit.

00:39.996 --> 00:41.620
So that's going to
be our solid and you

00:41.620 --> 00:48.630
can imagine that maybe this
right over here is our y-axis.

00:48.630 --> 00:50.000
That is our y-axis.

00:50.000 --> 00:56.310
And then going straight through
the center of the solid,

00:56.310 --> 00:59.230
you have your x-axis.

00:59.230 --> 01:01.120
If the solid was
slightly transparent,

01:01.120 --> 01:04.220
you would see the other side of
this opening right over here,

01:04.220 --> 01:06.990
and the x-axis goes
straight through the center.

01:06.990 --> 01:09.460
So how do we find the
volume of this solid?

01:09.460 --> 01:11.810
Well, we could think about
each of the individual disks

01:11.810 --> 01:14.105
of this solid right over here.

01:14.105 --> 01:15.230
So think about it this way.

01:15.230 --> 01:16.770
Let's take a disk
right over here.

01:16.770 --> 01:19.670
Let me take this
disk right over here

01:19.670 --> 01:22.300
so that you can imagine this
little bit of a cross section.

01:22.300 --> 01:25.280
It's a little bit of a
cross section of this solid.

01:25.280 --> 01:27.250
So this disk right
over here, it's

01:27.250 --> 01:30.010
going to have some area,
some surface area, and then

01:30.010 --> 01:31.589
it's going to have some depth.

01:31.589 --> 01:32.380
I'll draw the edge.

01:32.380 --> 01:33.970
You can imagine
the depth is kind

01:33.970 --> 01:36.820
of the edge of the quarter,
while the surface area is kind

01:36.820 --> 01:38.700
of the face of the
quarter, the area

01:38.700 --> 01:40.010
of the face of the quarter.

01:40.010 --> 01:42.710
So what's going to be the
volume of this white disk

01:42.710 --> 01:43.524
right over here?

01:43.524 --> 01:44.940
Well, it's going
to be the surface

01:44.940 --> 01:49.010
area of the disk
times the depth.

01:49.010 --> 01:51.110
So what is the surface area?

01:51.110 --> 01:55.165
Well, surface area of a circle
is pi times the radius squared.

01:57.820 --> 02:02.320
So it's going to be pi times--
and what's this radius?

02:02.320 --> 02:04.470
Well, the radius is the
height between the x-axis

02:04.470 --> 02:05.630
and the function.

02:05.630 --> 02:08.930
So this radius is
e to the 2x power.

02:08.930 --> 02:12.650
So the area of each
of these disks,

02:12.650 --> 02:15.520
for this x right over
here, for this value of x,

02:15.520 --> 02:21.315
is going to be pi times
e to the 2x squared.

02:21.315 --> 02:23.440
And then if we want the
volume of this entire disk,

02:23.440 --> 02:26.580
we then multiply the
area times the depth.

02:26.580 --> 02:29.070
So times dx.

02:29.070 --> 02:31.550
So this would be the volume
of this little disk over here

02:31.550 --> 02:35.540
that has a very small
infinitesimally wide depth.

02:35.540 --> 02:39.050
What we want to do is
sum all of these disks.

02:39.050 --> 02:40.810
We want to have a
bunch of these disks,

02:40.810 --> 02:42.274
that we want to
sum up all of them.

02:42.274 --> 02:43.690
So we want to take
an infinite sum

02:43.690 --> 02:46.010
of these infinitely thin disks.

02:46.010 --> 02:48.750
So essentially we're just going
to take the sum of all of these

02:48.750 --> 02:54.010
from x is equal to 0, all
the way to x is equal to k.

02:54.010 --> 02:56.080
Right, because this
right over here,

02:56.080 --> 02:58.780
this is x is equal to k,
this is x is equal to 0,

02:58.780 --> 03:00.250
we take the sum
of all the disks.

03:00.250 --> 03:02.069
We will get our actual volume.

03:02.069 --> 03:04.360
So we can actually evaluate
this integral analytically,

03:04.360 --> 03:05.780
it's not too bad.

03:05.780 --> 03:09.970
Because either--
let me rewrite it.

03:09.970 --> 03:16.190
Pi times-- e to the 2x squared
is e to the 4x, just 2x times

03:16.190 --> 03:17.040
2.

03:17.040 --> 03:18.990
And then, if we want
to take the integral,

03:18.990 --> 03:21.035
you might be able to do
this just by inspection,

03:21.035 --> 03:22.410
but if you want
to say, hey look,

03:22.410 --> 03:23.670
I have a little
function here, I want

03:23.670 --> 03:25.490
its derivative sitting
here, what we can do

03:25.490 --> 03:28.410
is we can multiply
this expression by 4,

03:28.410 --> 03:30.310
and we can also divide it by 4.

03:30.310 --> 03:32.320
If you multiply something
by 4 and divide by 4,

03:32.320 --> 03:33.569
you haven't changed its value.

03:33.569 --> 03:35.190
You've multiplied it by 1.

03:35.190 --> 03:37.210
But what this does
is it gives us--

03:37.210 --> 03:39.070
let me rewrite it a little bit.

03:39.070 --> 03:49.030
We can rewrite this as pi
over 4 times 4e to the 4x.

03:49.030 --> 03:50.100
I just rewrote this.

03:50.100 --> 03:53.529
I multiplied and divided by 4,
multiplied and divided by 4.

03:53.529 --> 03:55.320
And the reason why I
did that is so that we

03:55.320 --> 03:57.730
have the derivative of 4x
sitting right over here,

03:57.730 --> 03:58.860
we have this 4.

03:58.860 --> 04:01.320
Pi over 4 is a constant, so
we can actually take it out

04:01.320 --> 04:02.600
of the integral.

04:02.600 --> 04:05.160
And so we can say that this
expression right over here

04:05.160 --> 04:09.740
is the same as this
expression right over here,

04:09.740 --> 04:13.670
from pi over 4 times the
integral from 0 to k of 4e

04:13.670 --> 04:17.130
to the 4x dx.

04:17.130 --> 04:19.579
And so the whole reason why
I multiplied and divided by 4

04:19.579 --> 04:23.220
is I have something here,
I have its derivative.

04:23.220 --> 04:27.050
So I can really just pretend
like this is an e to the x

04:27.050 --> 04:32.700
and you can take the integral,
or the anti-derivative,

04:32.700 --> 04:34.610
with respect to 4x.

04:34.610 --> 04:38.360
So the inside is e to the 4x.

04:38.360 --> 04:39.250
And you can verify.

04:39.250 --> 04:41.000
If you take the
derivative of e to the 4x,

04:41.000 --> 04:44.820
it is the derivative of 4x,
which is 4, times e to the 4x.

04:44.820 --> 04:48.340
So this right here is the
anti-derivative of that.

04:48.340 --> 04:50.310
And we're going to evaluate it.

04:50.310 --> 04:52.930
We are going to
evaluate it from 0 to k.

04:55.500 --> 04:58.210
All of that times pi over 4.

04:58.210 --> 05:03.590
And so when you evaluate
it at k, it's e to the 4k.

05:03.590 --> 05:09.700
So this is going to be pi
over 4 times e to the 4k minus

05:09.700 --> 05:13.880
e to the 4 times 0, which is
e to the 0, which is minus 1.

05:13.880 --> 05:17.850
So this is our volume, pi over
4 times e to the 4k minus 1.

05:17.850 --> 05:19.970
So that is part b.

05:19.970 --> 05:21.180
We did that.

05:21.180 --> 05:21.890
Now part c.

05:21.890 --> 05:23.480
I think we have time for this.

05:23.480 --> 05:27.850
The volume, V, found in part
b, changes as k changes.

05:27.850 --> 05:31.730
If dk, the derivative of k
with respect to t is 1/3,

05:31.730 --> 05:33.760
determine the derivative
of V with respect

05:33.760 --> 05:35.830
to t when k is equal to 1/2.

05:35.830 --> 05:38.530
So this is straight up
from the chain rule.

05:38.530 --> 05:41.700
So the chain rule will tell us--
and if you view differentials

05:41.700 --> 05:43.150
as just very, very
small numbers,

05:43.150 --> 05:45.670
it actually seems like a
little bit of common sense--

05:45.670 --> 05:48.790
the rate of change of V,
so a very small change

05:48.790 --> 05:51.400
in V with respect to a
very small change in t

05:51.400 --> 05:55.910
is equal to a very small
change in V with respect

05:55.910 --> 06:02.840
to a very small change in k
times a very small change in k

06:02.840 --> 06:05.440
divided by a very
small change in t.

06:05.440 --> 06:07.770
Or the derivative of
V with respect to t

06:07.770 --> 06:10.270
is equal to the derivative
of V with respect to k times

06:10.270 --> 06:12.091
the derivative of k
with respect to t.

06:12.091 --> 06:13.840
And the reason why I
said this makes sense

06:13.840 --> 06:17.640
if you view a differential as
a small number is you would--

06:17.640 --> 06:20.206
and that's not a very
rigorous way of doing it,

06:20.206 --> 06:22.580
but it's a good kind of common
sense way of just thinking

06:22.580 --> 06:25.220
about things-- is that
these guys would cancel out

06:25.220 --> 06:26.820
if these were
numbers, and then you

06:26.820 --> 06:29.040
would have dV dt on both sides.

06:29.040 --> 06:31.610
So that's why I said that
this is slightly common sense.

06:31.610 --> 06:33.145
You can kind of
view it that way.

06:33.145 --> 06:35.520
But this essentially gives us
everything we need to know.

06:35.520 --> 06:38.230
We need to solve for dV dt.

06:38.230 --> 06:45.340
They already gave us dk dt at
1/3 when k is equal to 1/2.

06:45.340 --> 06:48.140
So they're already
telling us the rate

06:48.140 --> 06:50.602
of change of k
with respect to t.

06:50.602 --> 06:52.560
We already know that this
thing right over here

06:52.560 --> 06:54.790
is going to be 1/3.

06:54.790 --> 06:57.700
And we can figure out the
derivative of V with respect

06:57.700 --> 07:00.460
to k very easily because we
have V as a function of k right

07:00.460 --> 07:01.010
over here.

07:01.010 --> 07:02.600
So let's figure that out.

07:02.600 --> 07:07.850
So the derivative of
V with respect to k

07:07.850 --> 07:13.830
is equal to pi over 4 times
the derivative in here.

07:13.830 --> 07:16.160
And the derivative of
this is just 4e to the 4k.

07:16.160 --> 07:19.560
The derivative of
negative 1 is just 0.

07:19.560 --> 07:24.790
So this is times 4e to
the 4k, is the derivative.

07:24.790 --> 07:26.120
These guys cancel out.

07:26.120 --> 07:31.540
So it's equal to pi
e to the 4k, that's

07:31.540 --> 07:33.700
the derivative of V
with respect to k.

07:33.700 --> 07:37.270
We want to find it
when k is equal to 1/2,

07:37.270 --> 07:40.820
so we could write
V prime at 1/2,

07:40.820 --> 07:43.960
or when k is equal to 1/2 dV dk.

07:43.960 --> 07:50.020
dV dk is going to be equal to
pi times e to the 4 times 1/2

07:50.020 --> 07:53.430
which is equal to pi e squared.

07:53.430 --> 07:58.610
So this part right over
here is pi e squared.

07:58.610 --> 08:00.970
And so the derivative
of V with respect to t

08:00.970 --> 08:06.800
when k is equal to
1/2 and the derivative

08:06.800 --> 08:09.720
of k with respect to t is
1/3 is going to be just

08:09.720 --> 08:11.340
pi e squared times 1/3.

08:11.340 --> 08:13.280
So let me just
rewrite it over here.

08:13.280 --> 08:19.030
So dV dt is going to be
equal to 1/3 times this,

08:19.030 --> 08:23.490
or we could write
pi e squared over 3.

08:23.490 --> 08:25.440
And we're done.
